    "m.data.main": "<b>Main analysis.</b> {season}: {games} games and {shots} shots with coordinates (<code>ShotChartDetail</code>), plus the play-by-play with substitutions and score (<code>PlayByPlayV2</code>), from the NBA Stats API.",
    "m.data.def": "<b>Defense.</b> Our seasons do not include defender positions. For the simulator a second dataset is used: the 2014-15 shot log (about 128 thousand shots with defender distance, CC0 license), which measures how accuracy changes with how close the defender is. It is not merged with the other seasons.",
    "m.s1.h": "1 · Consecutive minutes (stints)",
    "m.s1.a": "Only game-clock time is counted. The counter continues between Q1 and Q2 and between Q3 and Q4, and resets at halftime.",
    "m.s1.b": "The play-by-play does not record substitutions between quarters: in each quarter we detect who was already on court and, if they came from the previous quarter, they keep their counter.",
    "m.s1.c": "Shots whose shooter did not appear on court ({x}) are excluded from the main analysis.",
    "m.s1.d": "Sensitivity check: reset the counter every quarter (see Evidence).",
    "m.s2.h": "2 · Expected shot (xShot)",
    "m.s2.p": "Probability of scoring given distance, position, zone, shot type and period; it uses neither minutes on court nor player identity.",
    "m.s2.val": "Validation: {v}.",
    "m.s2.model": "Chosen model: <b>{name}</b> · log-loss {ll} (no-model reference: {bl}) · AUC {auc} · calibration error {ece} pp.",
    "m.s2.temporal": "<b>Temporal test:</b> trained on earlier seasons and evaluated on {s} ({n} shots it never saw): log-loss {ll} (reference: {bl}), AUC {auc}, calibration {ece} pp.",
    "m.s3.h": "3 · Fatigue effect",
    "m.s3.a": "<b>Residual</b> = actual accuracy − xShot.",
    "m.s3.b": "<b>Per-player contrast</b> = mean residual with ≥{long} min − mean residual with {lo}–{short} min (already warmed up: this excludes the cold start, which is not fatigue). Subtracting within the same player removes their baseline, so each player is compared with themselves.",
    "m.s3.c": "<b>Population slope</b>: regression of the residual on consecutive minutes with player fixed effects and player-clustered robust errors.",
    "m.s3.d": "<b>Differences between players</b>: random-effects model (DerSimonian-Laird) → mean, τ and I².",
    "m.s3.e": "<b>Recommendation</b>: only if the 95% CI excludes 0 and the false discovery rate (Benjamini-Hochberg) is &lt; 5%.",
    "m.s3.f": "<b>Placebo test</b>: the procedure is repeated with minutes shuffled within each player; it measures how many recommendations appear when fatigue cannot exist.",
    "m.s4.h": "4 · New player",
    "m.s4.p": "Starting point: expected accuracy from their shot profile by zone plus their level. The fatigue effect comes from the population distribution (mean and τ). If own shots are entered, they are combined by precision (normal-normal Bayesian estimate).",
    "m.s5.h": "5 · Defense and 3D simulator",
    "m.s5.a": "<b>Rival team effect</b>: opponent accuracy minus what shot difficulty and the shooter predict, averaged per team and shrunk toward the league (empirical Bayes). The latest season is used.",
    "m.s5.b": "<b>Defender-distance effect</b>: monotone gradient boosting (more distance never lowers accuracy) on the 2014-15 data, with a confidence band from game resampling ({nb} replicates). The adjustment is relative to the typical defender distance for that shot type (≈5.5 ft on a three, ≈2.3 ft near the rim), because the shot-distance curve already includes how closely such shots are usually guarded. It is only offered between 1.5 and 8 ft, where there is enough data.",
    "m.s5.c": "<b>Simulator probability</b> = player base accuracy + shot-distance adjustment + defender effect + rival effect + cold start − fatigue. Every term comes from data; the positions of the other four defenders are illustrative.",
    "m.lim.h": "Limitations",
    "m.lim.a": "It is association, not causation: coaches substitute whoever they perceive as tired, so long stints are biased toward players who are doing well.",
    "m.lim.b": "No defender or individual fitness data in the main analysis.",
    "m.lim.b2": "In 2014-15, knowing the defender distance raises the expected-shot AUC from {a0} to {a1} ({a2} with clock, touches and dribbles): our model leaves that part as noise, and we cannot check whether it biases the comparison between minutes.",
    "m.lim.c": "The defender-distance effect comes from another era (2014-15) and measures association: defenders tend to be closer to the best shooters and there are fast breaks with no defender. The rival effect is the team average, not that of a specific defender.",
    "m.lim.d": "{n} seasons ({a} to {b}). Stints over 12 minutes are rare: beyond that, any projection is extrapolation.",
    "m.lim.e": "Limited power per player: the typical standard error of the contrast is {se} pp, so only individual drops of ≈{mde} pp or more would be detected. Smaller effects may or may not exist, but these data cannot show them.",
    "m.lim.f": "The thresholds ({lo}–{short} and {long} minutes) are a choice; the continuous slope is reported as an alternative.",
    "m.lim.g": "A result with no detectable effect does not prove fatigue does not exist: it means that, with these data, it cannot be told from zero. With {n} seasons the average is bounded fairly precisely, but not each player's.",

